          "In Linux, files are not merely names in folders; they are represented by an index node (inode). An inode stores all file metadata: file mode (permissions), owner UID, group GID, file size in bytes, timestamps (atime, mtime, ctime), link count, and pointers to the physical data blocks on the disk or NVMe drive. Crucially, the filename itself is NOT stored in the inode—filenames exist solely as directory entries (dentries) mapping a string name to an inode number. Step 2: Decode operands in 0x04: REG_A and 0x08: REG_B.",
          "Hard links allow multiple directory entries to point directly to the exact same inode number. Because they share the identical inode, modifying the content through one filename instantly updates all linked filenames with zero storage overhead. When a file is \"deleted\" with rm, the kernel simply decrements the inode reference count. Only when this link count drops to zero—and no active process has an open file descriptor referencing it—does the kernel deallocate the physical data blocks on disk.",
